,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,8,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,8,8,7,2,2,2],[7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,2,2,2],[7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,2,2,2],[7,7,7,7,7,7,3,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,2,2],[7,7,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,2,2],[7,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7],[7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7]],"output":[[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[3,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,8,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,8,8,7,2,2,2],[7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,2,2,2],[7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,2,2,2],[7,7,7,7,7,7,3,8,8,8,8,8,8,2,2,2],[7,7,7,7,7,7,8,3,8,8,8,8,8,2,2,2],[7,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,3,8,8,8,8,8,8,7],[7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8,8,8,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**Rule: what I see in the examples**\n\n- **Pair 1.** A 1‑rectangle overlaps a 4‑rectangle. In the input, colour 1 is drawn on top. In the output, the overlapping 2×4 block becomes 4. The rectangle that was underneath now shows in full.\n- **Pair 2.** A vertical 5‑line crosses a horizontal 9‑line. In the input, the crossing cell is 5. In the output, it is 9. The line that was underneath is now on top.\n\nSo the layering is reversed. Every shape that was partly hidden by another shape is completed, and its full outline is drawn over whatever covered it. Background cells never change. Only cells covered by another colour are repainted.\n\n**How to tell that a shape is hidden and what its full shape is**\n\n- **Rectangles.** Take a colour's bounding box. If every cell in the box is either that colour or another non‑background colour, and at least one is another colour, the shape is a partly covered rectangle. We complete it by painting the whole box.\n  - In pair 1, the 4 box (rows 4–9, columns 7–10) has 8 cells of 1 inside it, so it gets filled.\n  - The 1 box contains background cells at rows 4–5 of columns 7–10, so it is not touched.\n  - In pair 2, the 9 box (row 7, columns 2–11) holds one 5 cell, so it gets filled.\n  - The 5 box is entirely 5, so it stays as it is.\n- **Diagonal lines.** Test 2 has a 3‑line on the main diagonal that disappears under the large 8 triangle. If all cells of a colour lie on one diagonal, every non‑background cell between its two ends is painted with that colour.\n- **Shapes left alone.** The 8 triangle's bounding box contains background, so it is not a covered rectangle and is never repainted.\n\n**Applying this to the tests**\n\n- **Test 1.**\n  - The 9 band (rows 4–5) and the 1 band (rows 9–11) both have bounding boxes made entirely of non‑background cells. Both are covered rectangles, so they are drawn across the 5, 8 and 6 columns.\n  - The 5, 8 and 6 columns are already complete rectangles and stay the same.\n- **Test 2.**\n  - The 2 rectangle gets back its two cells at rows 7–8, column 13 that were covered by 8.\n  - The 3 diagonal is drawn through the 8 at (8,7), (9,8) and (10,9).","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    emit(out, \"Start from the input; find shapes partially hidden under others.\")\n    for c in sorted(set(np.unique(g)) - {bg}):\n        cells = list(zip(*np.where(g == c)))\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        box = g[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1]\n        if (box != bg).all() and (box != c).any():\n            sub = out[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1]\n            sub[box != bg] = c\n            emit(out, f\"Colour {c} is a covered rectangle: bring its full box to the front.\")\n            continue\n        for key in (lambda p: p[0]-p[1], lambda p: p[0]+p[1]):\n            if len(cells) > 1 and len({key(p) for p in cells}) == 1:\n                k = key(cells[0])\n                changed = False\n                for y in range(y0, y1+1):\n                    x = y - k if key is not None and key((1, 0)) == 1 and key((0, 1)) == -1 else k - y\n                    if 0 <= x < g.shape[1] and g[y, x] != bg and g[y, x] != c:\n                        out[y, x] = c\n                        changed = True\n                if changed:\n                    emit(out, f\"Colour {c} is a diagonal line passing under another shape: draw it on top.\")\n                break\n    emit(out, \"Final: hidden shapes now drawn in front.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"repair":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Start from the input; find shapes partially hidden under others."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Rectangles.** Take a colour's bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Shapes left alone.** The 8 triangle's bounding box contains background, so it is not a covered rectangle and is never repainted."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The 9 band (rows 4–5) and the 1 band (rows 9–11) both have bounding boxes made entirely of non‑background cells."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 1.** A 1‑rectangle overlaps a 4‑rectangle."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In the input, colour 1 is drawn on top."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In the output, the overlapping 2×4 block becomes 4."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"eeda4f5b386e7231265fd825e8bb5fb287407b12399969fc1172af26ec124a40","task_sha256":"0b8cd921927d7d338c945889e654a31b59b6689609c7c052be8a3201364e1d1e","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
